WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.818
A table in a data lake is often just a folder of Parquet files.

2
00:00:06.818 --> 00:00:11.524
To read the table, a reader lists the folder and reads every file in it.

3
00:00:11.733 --> 00:00:14.777
A nightly job adds two new files to the table.

4
00:00:14.777 --> 00:00:16.696
It writes them one at a time.

5
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:22.232
A reader that lists the folder right now sees the first new file, but not the second.

6
00:00:22.232 --> 00:00:23.612
It reads half a write.

7
00:00:23.833 --> 00:00:28.612
And if the job crashes now, the half-written batch stays in the table for good.

8
00:00:28.612 --> 00:00:31.697
A folder has no single moment when a write happens.

9
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:38.456
Iceberg fixes this with a small tree of metadata that names exactly which files belong to the table.

10
00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:41.649
At the bottom are the data files, as before.

11
00:00:41.649 --> 00:00:49.240
Above them, a manifest lists data files, with each file's row count and the minimum and maximum of every column.

12
00:00:49.467 --> 00:00:53.076
A manifest list names every manifest in one snapshot.

13
00:00:53.076 --> 00:00:56.072
A snapshot is the whole table at one moment.

14
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:00.856
The metadata file holds the table's schema and its list of snapshots.

15
00:01:00.856 --> 00:01:04.950
The catalog keeps one pointer: which metadata file is current.

16
00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:06.667
Every reader starts there.

17
00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:08.985
Now the job writes two new files.

18
00:01:08.985 --> 00:01:12.524
No reader sees them yet, because nothing points at them.

19
00:01:12.733 --> 00:01:21.935
The job writes a manifest for them, then a manifest list that reuses the old manifest, then a new metadata file that knows both snapshots.

20
00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:26.013
Then it swaps the catalog's pointer to the new metadata file.

21
00:01:26.013 --> 00:01:27.779
That one swap is the commit.

22
00:01:27.779 --> 00:01:30.869
A reader sees all of snapshot two, or none of it.

23
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:35.545
A reader that started a moment earlier still holds the old metadata file.

24
00:01:35.545 --> 00:01:39.625
It reads snapshot one to the end, and nothing it needs has changed.

25
00:01:39.833 --> 00:01:44.117
Old snapshots stay in the metadata, and their files stay in storage.

26
00:01:44.117 --> 00:01:47.959
So you can still ask for the table as it was in snapshot one.

27
00:01:47.959 --> 00:01:49.219
That is time travel.

28
00:01:49.433 --> 00:01:50.734
Files never change.

29
00:01:50.734 --> 00:01:58.193
Each commit adds a snapshot and moves one pointer, and every old snapshot stays readable until you expire it.
